{"question_id": "q01", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "What does the initial design phase do?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0001"], "reference_answer": "It characterizes fully the data needs of the prospective database users.", "notes": "Design Phases slide, first bullet."}
{"question_id": "q02", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "What is physical design in database design?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0002"], "reference_answer": "Physical design is deciding on the physical layout of the database.", "notes": "Design Phases (Cont.) slide, final bullet."}
{"question_id": "q03", "category": "definition", "question": "Which two major pitfalls must a database schema design avoid?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0003"], "reference_answer": "Redundancy, where repeated information can lead to inconsistency, and incompleteness, where some aspects of the enterprise become difficult or impossible to model.", "notes": "Design Alternatives slide lists exactly these two pitfalls."}
{"question_id": "q04", "category": "definition", "question": "Which design approaches does the chapter discuss?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0004"], "reference_answer": "The entity-relationship model, which models an enterprise as entities and relationships, and normalization theory, which formalizes what designs are bad and tests for them.", "notes": "Design Approaches slide."}
{"question_id": "q05", "category": "definition", "question": "What is an entity set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0006"], "reference_answer": "An entity set is a set of entities of the same type that share the same properties, for example the set of all persons or all companies.", "notes": "Entity Sets slide, second bullet."}
{"question_id": "q06", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which shape represents an entity set in an ER diagram, and how are its attributes shown?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0007"], "reference_answer": "Rectangles represent entity sets, attributes are listed inside the rectangle, and primary key attributes are underlined.", "notes": "Representing Entity sets in ER Diagram slide."}
{"question_id": "q07", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "What does a diamond represent in an ER diagram?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0010"], "reference_answer": "A diamond represents a relationship set.", "notes": "Representing Relationship Sets via ER Diagrams slide: 'Diamonds represent relationship sets.'"}
{"question_id": "q08", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which attribute is attached to the advisor relationship set in the worked example?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0011"], "reference_answer": "The attribute date, which tracks when the student started being associated with the advisor.", "notes": "Relationship Sets (Cont.) slide describing the advisor attribute date."}
{"question_id": "q09", "category": "definition", "question": "What is a role in the entity-relationship model?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0013"], "reference_answer": "When the entity sets of a relationship need not be distinct, each occurrence of an entity set plays a role in the relationship; the labels course_id and prereq_id are called roles.", "notes": "Roles slide."}
{"question_id": "q10", "category": "definition", "question": "What makes a relationship binary?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0014"], "reference_answer": "A binary relationship involves two entity sets, that is it has degree two; most relationship sets in a database system are binary.", "notes": "Degree of a Relationship Set slide."}
{"question_id": "q11", "category": "definition", "question": "What is a derived attribute and what example is given?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0016"], "reference_answer": "A derived attribute can be computed from other attributes; age can be derived given date_of_birth.", "notes": "Complex Attributes slide, derived attributes bullet."}
{"question_id": "q12", "category": "definition", "question": "What is the domain of an attribute?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0016"], "reference_answer": "The domain is the set of permitted values for each attribute.", "notes": "Same slide, last bullet."}
{"question_id": "q13", "category": "definition", "question": "How are composite attributes described?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0017"], "reference_answer": "Composite attributes allow attributes to be divided into subparts called component attributes, for example name split into first_name, middle_initial and last_name, or address split into street, city, state and postal_code.", "notes": "Composite Attributes slide."}
{"question_id": "q14", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which four mapping cardinality types exist for a binary relationship set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0019"], "reference_answer": "One to one, one to many, many to one, and many to many.", "notes": "Mapping Cardinality Constraints slide lists the four types."}
{"question_id": "q15", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "How do directed and undirected lines express cardinality constraints?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0022"], "reference_answer": "A directed line, an arrow, signifies one, while an undirected line signifies many, drawn between the relationship set and the entity set.", "notes": "Representing Cardinality Constraints in ER Diagram slide."}
{"question_id": "q16", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "What does a double line indicate in an ER diagram?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0026"], "reference_answer": "Total participation: every entity in the entity set participates in at least one relationship in the relationship set.", "notes": "Total and Partial Participation slide."}
{"question_id": "q17", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "In the minimum-maximum notation l..h, what does a maximum value of star mean?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0027"], "reference_answer": "A maximum value of star indicates no limit.", "notes": "Notation for Expressing More Complex Constraints slide."}
{"question_id": "q18", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "Why does the notation forbid more than one arrow leaving a ternary relationship?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0028"], "reference_answer": "Because with more than one arrow two different interpretations are possible, so the diagram would be ambiguous; to avoid confusion more than one arrow is outlawed.", "notes": "Cardinality Constraints on Ternary Relationship slide."}
{"question_id": "q19", "category": "definition", "question": "What is a key for an entity?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0030"], "reference_answer": "A key for an entity is a set of attributes that suffice to distinguish entities from each other.", "notes": "Primary key for Entity Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q20", "category": "definition", "question": "How is the primary key of a relationship set formed?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0031"], "reference_answer": "It is the union of the primary keys of the participating entity sets, plus any attributes associated with the relationship set; for advisor that is instructor.ID and student.ID.", "notes": "Primary Key for Relationship Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q21", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "What is the primary key of a many-to-many relationship set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0032"], "reference_answer": "The union of the primary keys is a minimal superkey and is chosen as the primary key.", "notes": "Choice of Primary key for Binary Relationship slide."}
{"question_id": "q22", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "For a one-to-one relationship, which side provides the primary key?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0032"], "reference_answer": "The primary key of either participating entity set forms a minimal superkey, so either one can be chosen.", "notes": "Same slide, one-to-one case."}
{"question_id": "q23", "category": "definition", "question": "What is a weak entity set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0034"], "reference_answer": "A weak entity set is one whose existence is dependent on another entity called its identifying entity; instead of a primary key it uses that identifying entity together with extra attributes called a discriminator.", "notes": "Weak Entity Sets (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q24", "category": "definition", "question": "What is a strong entity set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0035"], "reference_answer": "An entity set that is not a weak entity set is termed a strong entity set.", "notes": "Weak Entity Sets (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q25", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "How is a weak entity set drawn in an ER diagram?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0036"], "reference_answer": "A weak entity set is depicted via a double rectangle, its discriminator is underlined with a dashed line, and the identifying relationship is drawn as a double diamond.", "notes": "Expressing Weak Entity Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q26", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "Why is the dept_name attribute of student considered redundant?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0037"], "reference_answer": "Because it replicates information already present in the stud_dept relationship, so it is redundant and needs to be removed.", "notes": "Redundant Attributes slide."}
{"question_id": "q27", "category": "definition", "question": "How is an ER design turned into relation schemas?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0040"], "reference_answer": "Every entity set and relationship set is expressed as a relation schema with columns for its attributes, so the database becomes a collection of schemas.", "notes": "Reduction to Relation Schemas slide."}
{"question_id": "q28", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What happens to a weak entity set when it is reduced to a table?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0041"], "reference_answer": "It becomes a table that includes a column for the primary key of the identifying strong entity set, for example section(course_id, sec_id, sem, year).", "notes": "Representing Entity Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q29", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "How are composite attributes flattened into a relation schema?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0042"], "reference_answer": "A separate attribute is created for each component attribute, for example name_first_name and name_last_name, and the prefix can be omitted when unambiguous.", "notes": "Representation of Entity Sets with Composite Attributes slide."}
{"question_id": "q30", "category": "definition", "question": "How is a multivalued attribute represented in the relational model?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0043"], "reference_answer": "By a separate schema EM containing the primary key of the entity and the multivalued attribute, with one tuple per value; for example inst_phone(ID, phone_number).", "notes": "Representation of Entity Sets with Multivalued Attributes slide."}
{"question_id": "q31", "category": "definition", "question": "What schema represents a many-to-many relationship set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0044"], "reference_answer": "A schema with attributes for the primary keys of both participating entity sets plus any descriptive attributes, for example advisor(s_id, i_id).", "notes": "Representing Relationship Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q32", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "How can a many-to-one relationship that is total on the many side avoid its own schema?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0045"], "reference_answer": "By adding an extra attribute to the many side that holds the primary key of the one side, for example adding dept_name to the instructor schema instead of creating a schema for inst_dept.", "notes": "Redundancy of Schemas slide."}
{"question_id": "q33", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What problem can appear when a relationship schema is replaced by an extra attribute on a partially participating many side?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0046"], "reference_answer": "It could result in null values, because some entities on the many side do not participate in the relationship.", "notes": "Redundancy of Schemas (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q34", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "Why is the schema for the relationship between a weak entity set and its identifying strong entity set redundant?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0047"], "reference_answer": "Because the weak entity's own schema already contains the attributes that the relationship schema would hold, for example the section schema already contains what sec_course would contain.", "notes": "Redundancy of Schemas (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q35", "category": "definition", "question": "What is specialization in the extended ER model?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0049"], "reference_answer": "A top-down design process that designates sub-groupings within an entity set which become lower-level entity sets with attributes or relationships that do not apply to the higher-level set; it is drawn as a triangle labelled ISA and lower-level sets inherit attributes and relationship participation.", "notes": "Specialization slide."}
{"question_id": "q36", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What examples illustrate overlapping and disjoint specialization?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0050"], "reference_answer": "Overlapping is illustrated by employee and student, disjoint by instructor and secretary.", "notes": "Specialization Example slide."}
{"question_id": "q37", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What is the drawback of the first method for representing specialization as schemas?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0051"], "reference_answer": "Getting information about an employee requires accessing two relations, the one for the low-level schema and the one for the high-level schema.", "notes": "Representing Specialization via Schemas slide."}
{"question_id": "q38", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What is the drawback of the second method for representing specialization as schemas?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0052"], "reference_answer": "Redundancy: attributes such as name, street and city may be stored redundantly for people who are both students and employees.", "notes": "Representing Specialization as Schemas (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q39", "category": "definition", "question": "What is generalization?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0053"], "reference_answer": "A bottom-up design process that combines entity sets sharing the same features into a higher-level entity set; it is the simple inversion of specialization and is drawn the same way.", "notes": "Generalization slide."}
{"question_id": "q40", "category": "definition", "question": "What does a completeness constraint specify?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0054"], "reference_answer": "Whether an entity in the higher-level entity set must belong to at least one of the lower-level entity sets: total if it must, partial if it need not.", "notes": "Completeness constraint slide."}
{"question_id": "q41", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "How is a total generalization shown in an ER diagram?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0055"], "reference_answer": "By adding the keyword total and drawing a dashed line from it to the hollow arrow-head or arrow-heads it applies to; partial generalization is the default.", "notes": "Completeness constraint (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q42", "category": "definition", "question": "What does aggregation accomplish in an ER diagram?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0057"], "reference_answer": "It eliminates redundancy by treating a relationship as an abstract entity, which allows relationships between relationships.", "notes": "Aggregation (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q43", "category": "definition", "question": "How is aggregation reduced to relational schemas?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0059"], "reference_answer": "Create a schema containing the primary key of the aggregated relationship, the primary key of the associated entity set and any descriptive attributes, for example eval_for(s_ID, project_id, i_ID, evaluation_id); the proj_guide schema is then redundant.", "notes": "Reduction to Relational Schemas slide for aggregation."}
{"question_id": "q44", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "Why might phone be modelled as an entity instead of an attribute?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0063"], "reference_answer": "Because modelling phone as an entity allows extra information about phone numbers and supports multiple phone numbers.", "notes": "Entities vs. Attributes slide."}
{"question_id": "q45", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What guideline helps decide whether a concept should be an entity set or a relationship set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0064"], "reference_answer": "Designate a relationship set to describe an action that occurs between entities.", "notes": "Entities vs. Relationship sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q46", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "Why can modelling parents as two binary relationships beat one ternary relationship?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0065"], "reference_answer": "Because two binary relationships allow partial information, for example when only the mother is known.", "notes": "Binary Vs. Non-Binary Relationships slide."}
{"question_id": "q47", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "How can any non-binary relationship be replaced by binary relationships?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0066"], "reference_answer": "Create an artificial entity set E with three relationship sets RA, RB and RC linking it to A, B and C; give E an identifying attribute, move the attributes of R to E, and create one entity per relationship together with its three links.", "notes": "Converting Non-Binary Relationships to Binary Form slide."}
{"question_id": "q48", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What is the main limitation of converting a non-binary relationship into binary form?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0067"], "reference_answer": "Translating all constraints may not be possible, and the translated schema may contain instances that cannot correspond to any instance of the original relationship; making E a weak entity set avoids inventing an identifying attribute.", "notes": "Converting Non-Binary Relationships (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q49", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which design decisions are listed for ER modelling?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0068"], "reference_answer": "Attribute versus entity set, entity set versus relationship set, ternary versus a pair of binary relationships, strong versus weak entity set, specialization or generalization, and aggregation.", "notes": "E-R Design Decisions slide."}
{"question_id": "q50", "category": "definition", "question": "What is UML and how do its class diagrams relate to ER diagrams?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0073"], "reference_answer": "UML is the Unified Modeling Language, a language with many components for modelling aspects of a software system; its class diagrams correspond to ER diagrams but with several differences.", "notes": "UML slide."}
{"question_id": "q51", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "How are binary relationship sets drawn in UML class diagrams?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0076"], "reference_answer": "By drawing a line connecting the entity sets, with the relationship set name written adjacent to the line, and optionally the role name on the line next to the entity set.", "notes": "UML Class Diagrams (Cont.) slide."}
{"question_id": "q52", "category": "definition", "question": "What is a relationship set, stated mathematically?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0008"], "reference_answer": "A relationship set is a mathematical relation among n entities with n at least 2, each taken from an entity set: the set of tuples (e1, e2, ..., en) with each ei in Ei.", "notes": "Relationship Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q53", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "In a one-to-many advisor relationship between instructor and student, how many advisors can a student have?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0023"], "reference_answer": "A student is associated with at most one instructor via advisor, while an instructor is associated with several, possibly zero, students.", "notes": "One-to-Many Relationship slide."}
{"question_id": "q54", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which three cases of primary key choice does the chapter consider?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0029"], "reference_answer": "Entity sets, relationship sets, and weak entity sets.", "notes": "Primary Key slide."}
{"question_id": "q55", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "Why is the information in the sec_course relationship redundant for sections?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0033"], "reference_answer": "Because section already has an attribute course_id that identifies the related course; removing the relationship instead would make the connection implicit in an attribute, which is not desirable.", "notes": "Weak Entity Sets slide."}
{"question_id": "q56", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which chapter of Database System Concepts covers database design using the E-R model?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0000"], "reference_answer": "Chapter 6: Database Design Using the E-R Model.", "notes": "Title slide."}
{"question_id": "q57", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which ternary relationship example does the chapter use to discuss degree?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0015"], "reference_answer": "proj_guide, a ternary relationship between instructor, student and project.", "notes": "Non-binary Relationship Sets slide; also named on the degree slide."}
{"question_id": "q58", "category": "exact_terminology", "question": "Which other aspects of database design close the chapter?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0078"], "reference_answer": "Functional requirements, data flow and workflow, and schema evolution.", "notes": "Other Aspects of Database Design slide."}
{"question_id": "q59", "category": "paraphrase", "question": "What difference in cardinality depiction is noted between ER diagrams and UML class diagrams?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0074"], "reference_answer": "The position of the cardinality constraint depiction is reversed in UML relative to ER notation.", "notes": "ER vs. UML Class Diagrams slide footnote."}
{"question_id": "q60", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "What are composite attributes and how are they flattened when an entity set is reduced to a relation schema?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0017", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0042"], "reference_answer": "Composite attributes can be divided into subparts called component attributes, for example name into first_name and last_name; in a relation schema each component becomes its own attribute such as name_first_name and name_last_name.", "notes": "Definition on the Complex Attributes/Composite Attributes slides, flattening rule on the reduction slide."}
{"question_id": "q61", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "What is a multivalued attribute and how is it represented relationally?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0016", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0043"], "reference_answer": "A multivalued attribute can hold several values for one entity, such as phone_numbers; it is represented by a separate schema holding the entity's primary key plus the attribute, with one tuple per value.", "notes": "Attribute types on the Complex Attributes slide, representation rule on the multivalued slide."}
{"question_id": "q62", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "How is the primary key of a relationship set determined and how does the mapping cardinality change that choice?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0031", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0032"], "reference_answer": "It starts as the union of the participating entity sets' primary keys plus relationship attributes; for many-to-many that union is the minimal superkey, for one-to-many and many-to-one the primary key of the many side is used, and for one-to-one either side can be chosen.", "notes": "Primary Key for Relationship Sets plus Choice of Primary key for Binary Relationship."}
{"question_id": "q63", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "How is a weak entity set drawn in an ER diagram, and what columns does its table have after reduction?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0036", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0041"], "reference_answer": "It is drawn as a double rectangle with a dashed-underline discriminator and a double-diamond identifying relationship; its table includes a column for the primary key of the identifying strong entity set, as in section(course_id, sec_id, sem, year).", "notes": "Expressing Weak Entity Sets plus Representing Entity Sets."}
{"question_id": "q64", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "What is specialization and what is the drawback of representing it with one schema per entity set?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0049", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0051"], "reference_answer": "Specialization is a top-down process creating lower-level entity sets with attributes or relationships that do not apply to the higher-level set; the first schema method then needs two relations to answer a question about a low-level entity.", "notes": "Specialization plus method 1 drawback."}
{"question_id": "q65", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "How do total and partial participation differ and how can they be written with minimum-maximum constraints?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0026", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0027"], "reference_answer": "Total participation, drawn with a double line, means every entity participates in at least one relationship, while partial participation allows some entities to participate in none; in l..h notation a minimum of 1 indicates total participation and a maximum of 1 indicates at most one relationship.", "notes": "Total and Partial Participation plus Notation for Expressing More Complex Constraints."}
{"question_id": "q66", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "Why is aggregation introduced, and what schema does it produce?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0057", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0059"], "reference_answer": "Aggregation removes the redundancy between overlapping relationship sets by treating a relationship as an abstract entity, allowing relationships between relationships; the resulting schema holds the primary key of the aggregated relationship, the primary key of the associated entity set and descriptive attributes.", "notes": "Aggregation rationale plus its relational reduction."}
{"question_id": "q67", "category": "multi_passage", "question": "What is generalization and which completeness constraints can apply to it?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["udoc_151a82bc49d3:0053", "udoc_151a82bc49d3:0054"], "reference_answer": "Generalization is the bottom-up combination of entity sets that share features into a higher-level entity set; a completeness constraint says whether every higher-level entity must belong to a lower-level set, total if it must and partial if it need not.", "notes": "Generalization plus Completeness constraint."}
